<!--Post: 12667--><!--Time: 1112718976-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12667"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 05, 2005 - 9:36 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The funny thing I&#39;ve noticed is that even though Denny the Deadly Dog Catcher is under lock and key in his fashionable tangerine jumpsuit and comfy slippers, there are still theories about what he did and why out there that are, to be honest, ludicrous. We&#39;ve all run across posters on BTK messageboards who catch hold of some tangential portion of the story and use that to surmise that Denny did it all because he was part of some secret evil cult, or a lutheran offshoot of Opus Dei, or a closet member of cross-country traversing cross-dressing serial-killing underwear fetishists who like to drive tan vans and gold volkswagen bugs. <i>&#40;I made most of the preceding up to make a point.&#41;</i> <BR> <BR>So here&#39;s my fun idea. Even if you know that most of your previous ideas about BTK were logical, and now with the publication of &#34;Nightmare in Wichita&#34; you are finding you had some good sense after all, even if you&#39;ve been like me and read some of the nutty ideas and theories proposed and ranted on various boards over the last year and thrown up your hands in frustration, why not use this thread to <i>purposefully</i> concoct the most ludicrous BTK theories you can think of?  <BR> <BR>Even though April 1 has passed for the year, I think, why the heck not? <BR> <BR>Here&#39;s a couple of starters, based on theories I&#39;ve actually read in various places; <BR> <BR>- BTK was actually three people with names beginning B, T, and K. They committed all the murders for drug money. They framed Denny. <BR> <BR>- The Rader brothers all did it and framed Denny because he was the uptight one. <BR> <BR>- BTK is the mothman. <i>&#40;I read this somewhere, I swear.&#41;</i> <BR> <BR>Here&#39;s mine...caveat, again - this is purposefully whacky, <u>I do NOT believe this theory</u> - it&#39;s just for fun. <BR> <BR>BTK, Denny Rader, joins the military right out of high school, earlier than we&#39;re told. He is taken into a special black ops program for good sharpshooters, and on that November morning, you know the one, in Dallas, in 1963, Denny is the second shooter crouched on the grassy knoll.  <BR> <BR>Cut to &#39;68 - Denny&#39;s still in the service, but has been working his cover as a lowly radio tower construction guy - but he&#39;s called by his handlers to Los Angeles, to a Hotel where another Kennedy is giving a speech. They like Denny because in spite of his German heritage and midwestern appearance, he can easily be made ot look mediterranean. He&#39;s dressed in a busboy&#39;s outfit that night as he reaches around Sirhan, Denny&#39;s untraceable pistol in hand, and another member of clan Kennedy is down. <BR> <BR>A parking garage, Washington DC, 1973...Denny the Dog Catcher of the future, he&#39;s not yet BTK...he&#39;s giving this info to this reporter from the Post, a scruffy guy named Bernstein. Denny doesn&#39;t really like what he&#39;s doing, it&#39;s anti-Republican, but his handlers have been feeding him the info and he&#39;s being paid, so - <BR> <BR>January 1974, the pressure is too great, all this black ops stuff, and Denny does something awful... <BR> <BR>Gimme time and I could work the Mothman angle in there, too.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
