<!--Post: 12727--><!--Time: 1112932856-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12727"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, April 07, 2005 - 9:00 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I think the following is in keeping with this particular thread. <BR> <BR>A secret of mine is that I&#39;m a radio geek - as in, I am now and will always be an inveterate listener to both FM and AM radio, both music and talk - I don&#39;t have XM Satellite Radio yet, but it sounds like my idea of heaven. I&#39;m serious here - it&#39;s not a casual fancy. I find it&#39;s in part due to the fact that my discernment of information is sometimes heightened when it&#39;s only passed along through my ears. I can do crazy crap like name the announcer on most commercials, as well as tell you who is doing the voice-overs for a majority of the cartoons being made now - God knows the credits usually go by too fast to find out that way.  <BR> <BR>Anyhoo, my favorite radio show overall is the <a href="http://www.philhendrieshow.com">Phil Hendrie Show</a>, which is syndicated out of LA. Phil is easily, to my ear, the most brilliant comic talk host on the radio now and probably for years to come. He isn&#39;t afraid to wax political; and when he does, he&#39;s that truly rare bird, a left-leaning kinda libertarian-ish guy - but the best thing about Phil&#39;s show is the &#34;guests&#34; and the callers.  <BR> <BR>I really don&#39;t want to spoil the fun, if you haven&#39;t ever heard the show, as to what&#39;s so great about it, so I&#39;ll just paste below an e-mail I just sent to Phil for kicks; <BR> <BR><i>Hey Phil - <BR> <BR>I&#39;m one of those internet freaks who researches and writes &#40;blogs&#41; about crime, and I&#39;m obviously, as a Backstage Pass member, a huge fan of your show. <BR> <BR>I point the two out to back up my next point; how come Jay Santos hasn&#39;t taken up the cause of Dennis Rader, the Park City KS Compliance Officer accused of being the BTK Strangler?  <BR> <BR>As Compliance Officer, Rader is said to have; <BR> <BR>- measured people&#39;s grass with a ruler to see if it was within code, citing them when it wasn&#39;t. <BR> <BR>- stuck his head in one woman&#39;s kitchen window to give her a citation for some sort of codes infraction <BR> <BR>- generally been a complete and total ass - anal-retentive, unpleasant, stalker-ish... <BR> <BR>- but he was also, as I&#39;m sure you know, a Scoutmaster, president of his church congregation, and by many accounts, a &#34;good father&#34; - though I have my doubts. <BR> <BR>To my mind, it sounds like Dennis would have made the ULTIMATE Citizen&#39;s Auxiliary Policeman. I&#39;d figure Jay might be curious about this and see if a potential brother-in-arms is being railroaded simply because he did his Compliance Officer job with the same kind of zeal it seems Jay has for his self-appointed duties as an Auxiliary Policeman. <BR> <BR>And you know, I&#39;m going to try and &#39;shop a pith helmet onto Rader&#39;s mugshot, because I think Denny the Deadly Dog Catcher from Park City would like mighty good in those C.A.P. togs.  <BR> <BR>Whatcha think? <BR> <BR>&#40;Because I know you get mail from people who still don&#39;t &#34;get it&#34;, yeah, I know Jay is...um...some frightening portion of your own tortured and brilliant mind.&#41; <BR> <BR>I knew I was a fan for life, man, when I heard Margaret Gray go off on a caller one night with the following words; &#34;You know what you look like to me, with your good bag and your cheap shoes? You look like a rube...&#34; <BR> <BR>That was beautiful, Phil.  <BR> <BR>Keep up the 14 karat entertainment Phil, you are hands-down the best thing on &#34;talk&#34; radio today.  <BR> <BR> <BR>Sincerely, <BR> <BR>Steve Huff <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.planethuff.com/darkside">www.planethuff.com/darkside</a> <BR> <BR>&#34;Discourtesy is unspeakably ugly to me.&#34; ~ Hannibal Lecter</i> <BR> <BR>Now, if you know the joke on Phil&#39;s show, and if you&#39;re familiar enough to know who Jay Santos is, you will immediately see the correlation between the Citizens Auxiliary Police and Park City Compliance Officer.  <BR> <BR>As a matter of fact, to those of you who have listened to Phil&#39;s show, have any of you ever noticed how much Jay Santos <i>sounds</i> like Rader? I&#39;m thinking of the KSN video of him from 2000, when they interviewed him about dogs getting after cattle. That same flat, slightly high-pitched, nasal intonation.  <BR> <BR>I did my best to not phrase the letter like I was sending the guy an idea; he&#39;s got those aplenty, and they can&#39;t use listener-submitted &#34;ideas&#34; anyway. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12727-->
<!--Post: 12737--><!--Time: 1112968556-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12737"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lorenap<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lorenap<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 08, 2005 - 6:55 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve, I LOVE the Phil Hendrie show!!! I listen on my way to work sometimes. Dennis Rader is exactly what Jay Santos would look like!!! That comparison is too funny! Dennis Rader IS Santos! Let us know if you hear from him.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12737-->
<!--Post: 12739--><!--Time: 1112972394-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12739"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 08, 2005 - 7:59 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Lorenap; <BR> <BR>I must wax fanboy for a second and tell you that I DID get an e-mail back, and it was from Phil - since I&#39;m a paying member of his site, so I can listen to the show even if there is a sports event - local radio runs the Atlanta Braves, pre-empting Phil sometimes - the e-mail I wrote did go directly to Phil. The only thing cooler is receiving e-mail from Ann Rule and knowing she&#39;s read my blog. <BR> <BR>Turns out he&#39;s a true-crime afficionado himself, and he seemed a little floored by the news. Basically he said &#34;Jesus, I need to keep up with <i>that</i> story...&#34; <BR> <BR>That&#39;s all there is to tell at the moment, but it was pretty danged cool, if you ask me. I&#39;m glad another listener caught the comparison once I made it. Wouldn&#39;t Denny look good in the Citizens Auxiliary Police pith helmet? Where IS Major Elvis Newton when you need him?  <BR> <BR>Steve<!--/Text--></td></tr>
