<!--Post: 12760--><!--Time: 1113059234-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12760"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lostdog<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lostdog<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 09, 2005 - 8:07 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve and Lor, <BR> <BR>I hate to admit it.  <BR> <BR>Hendrie got me good the first time I heard him. <BR> <BR>Vic Prell – “Psychic to the Stars” was giving nonsensical readings over the airwaves to increasingly frustrated yet gullible callers. <BR>All the while “Vic” was hawking “star charts” to his listeners at ten dollars a pop. <BR> <BR>“HOW IN THE HELL DID THIS GUY GET ON THE RADIO!” <BR> <BR>Oh yes, I said that out loud, alone in my driver’s seat. <BR> <BR>It was a re-tooling of “Tele-Psychic”, one of Dan Akroyd’s brilliant bits from the golden age of SNL… <BR> <BR>And brother, I was fooled. <BR> <BR>I think that introduction to PH makes my occasional attendance to his program all the more enjoyable. <BR> <BR>He has reinvented and revitalized the once dying art of radio theater. <BR> <BR>Joe Frank <a href="http://www.joefrank.com/" target=_top>http://www.joefrank.com/</a>  <BR>Fresh Air withTerry Gross <BR>Ira Glass <BR>Selected Shorts <BR>Harry Shearers’s Le Show  <BR>The News From Lake Wobegon <BR> <BR>Before I discovered these wonderful dreamlands on the dial, I was jealous. <BR>Jealous of my parent’s wistful recollections of what radio signified in their youth. <BR> <BR>That rich pastiche of human emotion and imagination is still with us today… <BR>If you know where to look… and listen. <BR> <BR> <BR>NipperDog <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12760-->
<!--Post: 12762--><!--Time: 1113060394-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12762"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 09, 2005 - 8:26 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Lost; <BR> <BR>I first heard Bobbie and Steve Dooley talking with Phil about how the Western Estates Homeowners Assoc. had passed an ordinance that residents were allowed to only have satellite television, as the dishes provided for a cool futuristic look. <BR> <BR>I was furious, initially - what the HELL was wrong with these people? And Bobbie, with her bland pronouncements that just kept getting more and more ludicrous as she responded to each real caller. <BR> <BR>Finally my sense of reality testing was blown all to hell, and I realized - aw hell, it&#39;s a <i>show.</i> So I caught on fast, and have been hooked ever since.  <BR> <BR>In fact Phil inspired me, in a bizarre way. At ks.com in particular, as I used a new nic, I realized I was, a la Phil Hendrie, making up a unique and weird persona for each name. Larry Anderson - avuncular, hail-fellow-well-met, smart, insightful, funny - a_bracksis - a smartass, sarcastic, spoiling for a fight, eccentric - thundersaid - like &#34;Scroll&#34; from that board, if you read ks.com you know who I mean, they go by Squall now, only thundersaid dared to actually make sense. He was part beat poet, part oracle, part provocateur.  <BR> <BR>No one seemed to catch on that all three were one guy until I told them, whereupon I was greeted with reactions ranging from &#34;you&#39;re seriously disturbed&#34; to &#34;that&#39;s effin&#39; hilarious.&#34; <BR> <BR>Which is probably a lot like what Phil gets. Amazing to me, too, the vast fund of callers Phil always gets who just.don&#39;t.get. it. <BR> <BR>Now, I&#39;m hoping Jay Santos of the Citizens Auxiliary Police will jump on that &#34;Free Denny&#34; bandwagon and get his anal-retentive brother-in-arms sprung. I could see Jay saying something like, &#34;even if he did kill those people, they probably just weren&#39;t up to code.&#34; <BR> <BR>Demented and sad, but funny. Like my life. <BR> <BR>Steve<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12762-->
<!--Post: 12767--><!--Time: 1113075118-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12767"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lostdog<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lostdog<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 09, 2005 - 12:31 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve, <BR> <BR>From the moment I read your first Larry Anderson post, I imagined he looked like Herbert Anderson, the actor who played Dennis the Menace’s dad on the early 60s sitcom. <BR> <BR>Hmmmmmm… Dennis the Meni… DAMMIT! There was a clue there all along. <BR> <BR>Please, tell me you have listened to Joe Frank. <BR> <BR>LD <BR> <BR>. . . . . . . . . . . . . <BR> <BR>Right then, on to my crackpot theory. <BR> <BR>Dennis Rader will never forget the day he realized that only he could hear the dogs talk. <BR> <BR>“Dennis! Come quickly! Old man Caruthers has fallen into the well!” <BR> <BR>Dennis grabbed a flashlight from the shed and hurried after X-ray, his neighbor’s Shepard-Collie mix. <BR> <BR>“Lower the rope s-son! That’s a g-good boy,” cried the old man. “It’s c-c-cold.” <BR> <BR>X-ray was bounding about the boy, barking up a stringy froth.  <BR>“What are you waiting for Dennis!  <BR> <BR>DENNIS! You’re the only one here with thumbs! Do something!” <BR> <BR>The boy was motionless, transfixed.  <BR>He trembled as the flashlight tumbled down the shaft - - light bouncing off of stone and water and the old man’s eyes. <BR> <BR>Walking away Dennis ignored X-ray’s pleading. <BR>The last thing Caruthers heard was a plaintive howl vanishing in the distance. <BR> <BR>. . . . . . . . . . . . .   <BR> <BR>Tonight, a KAKE exclusive: <BR> <BR>“Acting on an exhumation warrant, Salina police today unearthed the Fifty-three year-old remains of the person purported to be the true biological mother of confessed BTK killer Dennis Lynn Rader. <BR> <BR>While security at the gravesite was tight, KAKE news has learned of a shocking discovery. <BR> <BR>You’ll hear about it tonight, only on KAKE. <BR> <BR>We join Jeanene Kiesling live in front of the Holy Sepulcher Cemetery, just outside of Salina, for this KAKE exclusive report. Jeanene?” <BR> <BR>“Thank you Susan. <BR> <BR>Susan, while authorities have refused to disclose a name, sources have confirmed that the remains exhumed this afternoon under the watchful eyes of the Salina P.D., Wichita police and the KBI… do not appear to be human. <BR> <BR>As you can see in this footage obtained by KAKE news, a forensics team appears to be examining the skeletal remains of a large breed of dog at the gravesite. <BR> <BR>With us is Dr. Henry Mitchell, a local veterinarian. <BR> <BR>Dr. Mitchell, can you describe for us what we are seeing here?” <BR> <BR>“Well, I’ll do my best. <BR>Although I haven’t seen the remains myself, the film appears to show a bone structure resembling that of a full-grown Doberman Pinscher… probably a bitch…” <BR> <BR>Queue the choir… and fade to black. <BR> <BR> <BR>DarkDog     <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12767-->
<!--Post: 12770--><!--Time: 1113082355-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12770"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 09, 2005 - 2:32 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><i>&#34;I imagined he looked like Herbert Anderson, the actor who played Dennis the Menace’s dad on the early 60s sitcom.&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>Then you pretty much nailed it, LD. Add some gray at the temples and a little potbelly, a shirt pocket never without pens, and you&#39;ve got Larry. He has the voice of one of our local traffic announcers here in ATL, Art &#34;Madman&#34; Mehring, too. Should be grating, but is actually friendly and interesting to listen to.  <BR> <BR>Mind you, I&#39;m a 6&#39;0&#34; redhead with thinning hair, a goatee, never tuck in my shirts unless I&#39;m in church and I wear contacts most of the time. In short, Larry looks nothing like me, in my mind or yours. <BR> <BR>a_bracksis looked a lot like Anton LaVey, and though when I shaved my head I was accused of looking like the former head of the Church of Satan myself, I still don&#39;t look like a_bracksis.  <BR> <BR>thundersaid was an entity that could not be seen, only sensed and felt. He did, however, have a thing for bagels. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12770-->
